@@ -0,0 +1,153 @@
+# Xbox IRP transport contract
+
+This contract freezes public behavior before the Xbox IRP port. It describes
+observable results and does not prescribe the internal implementation.
+
+## Sources and boundary
+
+- Public ABI declarations: XboxDev/nxdk commit
+  `73c95900965a16be3a3e34b8d4d5d41bc18498be`,
+  `lib/xboxkrnl/xboxkrnl.h`.
+- Black-box behavior: the public `irp-public-shape` XBE run against Complex,
+  selected SpiritBoot 0168, and the rejected logical-size-only candidate on
+  2026-10-18.
+- Target source: SpiritBoot's independently maintained Xbox I/O manager and
+  storage path at selected patch 0168.
+
+No proprietary implementation source or disassembly is an input.
+
+## Public packet layout
+
+- `sizeof(IRP)` is 104 bytes and its alignment is eight bytes.
+- `IoStatus`, `StackCount`, `UserBuffer`, `SegmentArray`,
+  `LockedBufferLength`, `Tail.Overlay.CurrentStackLocation`, and
+  `Tail.Overlay.OriginalFileObject` remain at public offsets 0x10, 0x18,
+  0x30, 0x34, 0x38, 0x5c, and 0x60 respectively.
+- `sizeof(IO_STACK_LOCATION)` is 36 bytes.
+- `IoAllocateIrp(n)` reports `Size = 104 + 36*n` and initially points
+  `CurrentStackLocation` exactly one byte past that logical packet.
+- `IoInitializeIrp(packet, 104 + 36*n, n)` clears no byte past the supplied
+  packet, reports that exact size, and points the current stack to the packet
+  end. The aligned padding dword at public offset 0x64 is zero after allocation
+  and public initialization; kernel bookkeeping does not consume it.
+
+## Public flags
+
+The Xbox flag values are the nxdk values. In particular, 0x200 is
+`IRP_UNLOCK_USER_BUFFER`; it is not the NT `IRP_WRITE_OPERATION` value.
+NT-only bookkeeping must not leak through the title-visible `Flags` word.
+
+## FSD request builders
+
+For a one-stack title-owned device and a 512-byte request, Complex 4627 returns
+a 140-byte packet and preserves the caller buffer in `UserBuffer` for every
+transfer method. A neither-I/O or `DO_DIRECT_IO` device exposes
+`SegmentArray = NULL`, `LockedBufferLength = 0`, and public `Flags = 0`. A
+`DO_BUFFERED_IO` device exposes `SegmentArray = NULL`,
+`LockedBufferLength = Length`, and public `Flags = IRP_UNLOCK_USER_BUFFER`
+(0x200). The synchronous read builder places the current stack at offset 104
+while the title dispatch routine owns the request. The three-mode oracle is
+byte-identical on the historical and current release xemu builds, so these
+differences belong to the firmware contract rather than the emulator.
+
+The title export contract and the kernel's compatibility transport are
+separate. The exported builder publishes the native fields above. A public
+`DO_DIRECT_IO` request also carries a hidden MDL so it can be forwarded from a
+title-owned device into a kernel storage stack. A public `DO_BUFFERED_IO`
+request exposes its pinned `UserBuffer` through the private SystemBuffer
+accessor as the same allocation; it must not carry a second bounce buffer whose
+completion copy could overwrite changes made by a title dispatch routine.
+Internal kernel calls to `IoBuild*FsdRequest` retain the private
+direct/buffered/neither transport and do not publish title-only pins. Terminal
+completion must release each selected private transport and public page pin
+exactly once. Failure before ownership transfer must leave no MDL, buffer,
+page lock, or packet allocation live.
+
+## Internal compatibility
+
+Kernel allocation, lookaside reuse, quota accounting, reserve IRPs, associated
+IRPs, cancellation, buffered control requests, paging I/O, APC delivery, MDL
+ownership, and scatter/gather requests must keep their existing observable
+behavior without adding fields to the public packet or moving its stack.
+Private NT compatibility state must have an explicit lifetime independent of
+title-visible packet bytes.
+
+Caller-owned packets initialized with `IoInitializeIrp` provide the public
+packet and stack allocation. When a kernel path needs cancellation, an MDL, or
+buffered-I/O state, the I/O manager attaches separate kernel-only state lazily.
+This state never changes allocation provenance: the I/O manager may clean the
+attached resources, but it must not free the caller's packet. A completion
+routine may retain, reinitialize, or resubmit that packet by returning
+`STATUS_MORE_PROCESSING_REQUIRED`; cleanup must distinguish those lifecycles.
+
+## Failure diagnosis and repair
+
+The rejected port used a 128-byte NT-shaped header in a 104-byte Xbox ABI.
+That moved every stack location by 24 bytes and exposed NT flag values through
+the Xbox `Flags` word. A later builder rewrite then forced every FSD request
+through an invented public pinned-buffer transport, ignoring the target
+device's buffered/direct/neither method. Games then parsed the wrong stack
+fields or received no MDL where the storage path required one. The result was
+intermittent disc data corruption, missing Halo and Forza menu assets, delayed
+Forza world population, and terminal completion/resource failures.
+
+The repaired design keeps the 104-byte packet exact and places 80 bytes of
+kernel state before allocations made by `IoAllocateIrp`. Allocator metadata,
+the pool tag, and an address-bound signature prove ownership; mutable public
+`Type`, `Size`, and `StackCount` validate packet shape but never decide lifetime.
+The prefix records the original pinned range and the small/large lookaside class,
+so public field mutation cannot leak a pin or return memory to the wrong cache.
+
+The native 48-byte scheduler `KAPC` lives in the private prefix. It is never
+overlaid on the public 40-byte Xbox APC view, which would overwrite the first
+four bytes of the public stack. Completion resolves the private state once,
+snapshots the caller-visible fields, performs cleanup and APC setup, and makes
+event signaling the final packet lifetime boundary. A caller may therefore
+release its packet immediately after observing the event without racing a later
+kernel access.
+
+Whole-page IRP ownership uses one tag slot per page in the 16 MiB pool window.
+There is no 128-live-allocation ceiling, and a legacy metadata table must roll
+an allocation back rather than return memory that cannot later be identified.
+`ExQueryPoolBlockSize` recognizes the public pointer inside an owned IRP and
+returns only the safe caller-visible capacity after the private prefix.
+
+`NXK_MM_POOLPAGES` is now required for Xbox builds. Its page-state map is what
+lets the kernel reject an arbitrary caller pointer before inspecting bytes at a
+negative offset.
+
+## Executable evidence
+
+- `tests/host/irp-private-lifetime` compiles the production lookup, validation,
+  initialization, pin-release, reuse, and lookaside-selection bodies under
+  AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. It mutates every public
+  ownership-looking field and verifies that allocator ownership survives while
+  shape validation fails independently. Poisoned caller buffers prove that
+  `IoInitializeIrp` writes exactly the supplied packet.
+- `tests/host/io-apc-order` compiles the production Xbox terminal-completion
+  body and covers 74 success, error, buffered, user-APC, delivery, rundown, and
+  immediate waiter-free cases. Its 122 owned packets, 50 MDLs, and 25 buffers
+  are each released exactly once.
+- `tests/host/irp-direct-builder` compiles the production FSD builder and both
+  completion cleanup paths. It proves all three transfer methods, fault
+  cleanup, the direct-read/write MDL access direction, and rejection of a
+  mutation that removes the direct-I/O branch.
+- `tests/host/pool-tagged-interior` executes small-bucket and whole-page
+  ownership resolution for ordinary, census, title-domain, and disabled-page
+  configurations.
+- The nxstorage, XDVDFS, pool, MDL-pin, upper-memory, and thread-view production
+  fixtures exercise the consumers changed by the port.
+
+## Acceptance checks
+
+1. Target layout and flag assertions compile for release i386.
+2. Production-body host fixtures pass under AddressSanitizer and
+   UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, including poison boundaries around caller-owned
+   packets.
+3. Mutations that restore a 128-byte header, NT flag values, an NT buffered
+   builder, an out-of-packet stack pointer, or an unmatched pin fail.
+4. The public XBE matches the frozen Complex table for allocation,
+   initialization, and builders.
+5. The full host suite and release64 firmware build pass before any title gate.
+6. Cold automated PGR2, Halo, DOA, and Forza title gates pass before a firmware
+   image is offered for manual testing.
