--- /dev/null
+++ b/tests/xbe/cold-storage-persistence/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
+# Cold storage persistence regression
+
+This product-owned XBE turns one disposable FATX HDD overlay into a two-phase
+cold-restart oracle. It does not use a save or HDD authored by another BIOS.
+
+On a disk without `SpiritBootColdManifest.bin`, phase A overwrites a 2 MiB
+payload and a 2 MiB spacer with 50 alternating, sector-aligned, noncached
+writes. The alternating allocation makes the two files compete for adjacent
+clusters; the payload also spans many storage packets even if its final FATX
+chain happens to remain contiguous. Phase A flushes and closes both files,
+writes its 28-byte checksum-protected manifest last, flushes the volume, emits
+one `COLD_STORAGE_RESULT phase=A status=PASS ...` serial line, and powers off.
+
+Start a new xemu process with the same BIOS, EEPROM, and writable HDD overlay.
+Do not use `-snapshot`. Phase B reopens the manifest and payload, validates the
+exact file length and every generated byte first through synchronous noncached
+reads and then through asynchronous event-completed noncached reads. It
+requires at least one `STATUS_PENDING` request, emits one phase-B result line,
+and powers off. The files stay on disk so later cold boots repeat phase B. Use a
+fresh overlay to author another phase A.
+
+Build and run the host contract:
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/host/cold-storage-persistence/check.py
+NXDK_DIR=/home/codex/src/nxdk \
+  PATH=/home/codex/src/nxdk/bin:$PATH \
+  make -C tests/xbe/cold-storage-persistence clean all
+```
+
+After the runner has produced separate cold-boot serial captures, grade them:
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/xbe/cold-storage-persistence/grade.py \
+  phase-a-serial.log phase-b-serial.log
+```
+
+The grader requires phase A and B to pass, requires their byte counts and
+digests to match, and rejects a phase-B run that did not exercise synchronous,
+asynchronous, and pending completion paths.
